<!--Post: 2584--><!--Time: 1103827238-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2584"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->J<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->J<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 23, 2004 - 10:40 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Correct me if I&#39;m wrong, but my understanding is that someone THIS YEAR brought up the fact that Wyatt used the lyrics of &#34;Oh Death&#34; in her classes and, since BTK had a chapter titled &#34;PJ&#39;s&#34;, the WPD decided this meant he had a connection with her - and his adaptation of &#34;Oh Death&#34; for his Fox poem was because of this. <BR> <BR>Unless there is actual evidence to suggest BTK knew P.J. Wyatt, I&#39;d say there is likely no connection and this angle is a wild good chase. <BR> <BR>&#34;O Death&#34; was a traditional folk/spiritual song that I would assume reached many more people as a &#42;song&#42; than as a poem. &#40;You can catch a very nice rendition of it in the 2000 movie &#34;O Brother, Where Art Thou?&#34;&#41;.  And, if it&#39;s true that BTK&#39;s grandfather played the fiddle &#40;a musical guy&#41;, I&#39;d suggest it would be much more likely that BTK was exposed to the song &#34;O Death&#34; as a child and that&#39;s why it was easy for him to adapt the lyrics for his Fox poem. He knew the song by heart due to much repetition in childhood &#40;more believable than knowing a poem by heart from a college class.&#41; <BR> <BR>As for &#34;PJ&#39;s&#34; being a chapter title, I think this could refer to many things.  I don&#39;t think it has to be only a reference to a person named P.J. and, even if it is, why Wyatt?  Keep in mind, too, that BTK is not a gifted writer.  He often does not use proper spelling or punctuation.  He may well have been making the mistake many do when pluralizing initials or years.  For example, to pluralize one should write &#34;80s&#34;, not &#34;80&#39;s&#34;  and &#34;TDs&#34; not &#34;TD&#39;s&#34; &#40;for touchdowns&#41;.  So, &#34;PJ&#39;s&#34; could correctly refer to the possessive of P.J. &#40;but not necessarily P.J. Wyatt&#41; - OR &#34;PJ&#39;s&#34; could be the INCORRECT way of writing the plural of anything represented by &#34;PJ&#34; &#40;pajamas, &#34;private joke&#34;, etc....&#41;. <BR> <BR>In short - as with many ideas that get posted on this message board - I just don&#39;t think there&#39;s a clear reason to work the P.J. Wyatt angle. &#40;UNLESS, of course, there are more details that have not been released by WPD!&#41; <BR> <BR>Now....if he adapted &#34;O Death&#34; for his poem about Nancy, then I&#39;d suggest he may have borrowed from another song to do the poem about Anna.  I know this has been researched somewhat, but I suspect enough word changing may have occurred that the link may be harder to find. Note, for example, he uses the word &#34;rapture&#34; twice and &#34;enrapture&#34; once....all in the span of a few lines. This isn&#39;t a very commonly used word; I&#39;ll bet he borrowed it from someone much more talented. <BR> <BR>In the end, though, I doubt that learning more about these songs/poems will actually lead to identifying BTK.  I just think it&#39;s more valuable to concentrate on things he actually wrote than dealing with coded messages submitted by BTK posers on the boards.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
